dget_type=\"text-editor.default\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-widget-container\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<p>Need something cleared up? Here are our most frequently asked questions.<\/p>\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-37add73 elementor-widget__width-initial elementor-widget elementor-widget-jkit_accordion\" data-id=\"37add73\" data-element_type=\"widget\" data-e-type=\"widget\" data-widget_type=\"jkit_accordion.default\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-widget-container\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<div  class=\"jeg-elementor-kit jkit-accordion style-default jeg_module__11_69ff268cb73a4\" ><div class=\"card-wrapper \">\n                <div class=\"card-header\">\n                    <a href=\"#expand-d49999f\" class=\"card-header-button\" aria-expanded=\"false\" data-target=\"#expand-d49999f\" aria-controls=\"expand-d49999f\">\n                        <div class=\"left-icon-group\">\n                <div class=\"normal-icon\"><i aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"jki jki-plus-light\"><\/i><\/div>\n                <div class=\"active-icon\"><i aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"jki jki-minus-light\"><\/i><\/div>\n            <\/div><span class=\"title\">How long does frozen shoulder last?<\/span>\n                    <\/a>\n                <\/div>\n                <div class=\"card-expand\" id=\"expand-d49999f\" style=\"display:none\"><div class=\"card-body\"><p>How long does frozen shoulder last depends on treatment. Natural history untreated: 18-24 months typically (some cases 2-3 years). With proper frozen shoulder treatment: 6-12 months to 80-90% recovery. Frozen shoulder recovery time accelerated by: consistent daily exercises, weekly physical therapy, corticosteroid injections, early treatment. Diabetic frozen shoulder typically lasts longer 18-24 months even with treatment. About 10-15% have mild permanent residual stiffness. Frozen shoulder healing time shortened significantly when treatment started early in Stage 1 (freezing phase).<\/p><\/div><\/div>\n            <\/div><div class=\"card-wrapper \">\n                <div class=\"card-header\">\n                    <a href=\"#expand-339abdc\" class=\"card-header-button\" aria-expanded=\"false\" data-target=\"#expand-339abdc\" aria-controls=\"expand-339abdc\">\n                        <div class=\"left-icon-group\">\n                <div class=\"normal-icon\"><i aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"jki jki-plus-light\"><\/i><\/div>\n                <div class=\"active-icon\"><i aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"jki jki-minus-light\"><\/i><\/div>\n            <\/div><span class=\"title\">What are the best frozen shoulder exercises?<\/span>\n                    <\/a>\n                <\/div>\n                <div class=\"card-expand\" id=\"expand-339abdc\" style=\"display:none\"><div class=\"card-body\"><p>Best exercises for frozen shoulder: (1) External rotation stretch most critical as most restricted motion. Doorway stretch or stick assisted. Hold 30-60 seconds, 3 sets 3x daily. (2) Wall walks progressive forward flexion stretch. Mark progress weekly. (3) Pendulum exercises gentle gravity assisted motion. Safe for all stages. (4) Overhead pulley use good arm to assist affected arm. (5) Cross body stretch for posterior capsule. Frozen shoulder stretches must be performed daily consistently missing days significantly delays recovery. Frozen shoulder exercises should take shoulder to discomfort (not sharp pain). Work with physical therapist for proper technique. Adhesive capsulitis exercises require months of consistent performance for best results.<\/p><\/div><\/div>\n            <\/div><div class=\"card-wrapper \">\n                <div class=\"card-header\">\n                    <a href=\"#expand-cfd3e25\" class=\"card-header-button\" aria-expanded=\"false\" data-target=\"#expand-cfd3e25\" aria-controls=\"expand-cfd3e25\">\n                        <div class=\"left-icon-group\">\n                <div class=\"normal-icon\"><i aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"jki jki-plus-light\"><\/i><\/div>\n                <div class=\"active-icon\"><i aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"jki jki-minus-light\"><\/i><\/div>\n            <\/div><span class=\"title\">Can frozen shoulder heal without treatment?<\/span>\n                    <\/a>\n                <\/div>\n                <div class=\"card-expand\" id=\"expand-cfd3e25\" style=\"display:none\"><div class=\"card-body\"><p>Yes, frozen shoulder treatment at home or with medical care, the natural history is eventual resolution in most cases. Untreated frozen shoulder typically lasts 18-24 months before spontaneous improvement. However, 10-15% have permanent residual stiffness without treatment. Benefits of treatment vs untreated: Faster recovery (6-12 months vs 18-24 months), Less severe stiffness at peak, Better final outcome (less residual restriction), Reduced pain during freezing stage, Maintained function during recovery. How to treat frozen shoulder conservatively accelerates recovery significantly daily stretching, physical therapy, injections can reduce duration by 50-60%. Best treatment for frozen shoulder starts early in Stage 1 to minimize progression.<\/p><\/div><\/div>\n            <\/div><div class=\"card-wrapper \">\n                <div class=\"card-header\">\n                    <a href=\"#expand-751ef88\" class=\"card-header-button\" aria-expanded=\"false\" data-target=\"#expand-751ef88\" aria-controls=\"expand-751ef88\">\n                        <div class=\"left-icon-group\">\n                <div class=\"normal-icon\"><i aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"jki jki-plus-light\"><\/i><\/div>\n                <div class=\"active-icon\"><i aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"jki jki-minus-light\"><\/i><\/div>\n            <\/div><span class=\"title\">What causes frozen shoulder to develop?<\/span>\n                    <\/a>\n                <\/div>\n                <div class=\"card-expand\" id=\"expand-751ef88\" style=\"display:none\"><div class=\"card-body\"><p>What causes frozen shoulder remains incompletely understood, but known risk factors and triggers: Diabetes (20-30% of diabetics vs 2-5% general population)\u2014strongest risk factor. Frozen shoulder and diabetes strongly linked, likely due to glycosylation of capsular collagen. Shoulder injury or surgery requiring immobilization. Rotator cuff tear or repair. Stroke causing hemiplegia and disuse. Systemic conditions: thyroid disorders, Parkinson's, cardiovascular disease. Prolonged shoulder immobility from any cause. Age 40-60 (peak incidence). Female gender slightly higher. Idiopathic (30-40% occur spontaneously without identifiable trigger). Frozen shoulder causes involve inflammatory cascade leading to capsular thickening and contraction.<\/p><\/div><\/div>\n            <\/div><div class=\"card-wrapper \">\n                <div class=\"card-header\">\n                    <a href=\"#expand-fdae1f4\" class=\"card-header-button\" aria-expanded=\"false\" data-target=\"#expand-fdae1f4\" aria-controls=\"expand-fdae1f4\">\n                        <div class=\"left-icon-group\">\n                <div class=\"normal-icon\"><i aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"jki jki-plus-light\"><\/i><\/div>\n                <div class=\"active-icon\"><i aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"jki jki-minus-light\"><\/i><\/div>\n            <\/div><span class=\"title\">What is the difference between frozen shoulder and shoulder impingement?<\/span>\n                    <\/a>\n                <\/div>\n                <div class=\"card-expand\" id=\"expand-fdae1f4\" style=\"display:none\"><div class=\"card-body\"><p>Key differences: Range of motion frozen shoulder has severely restricted passive ROM (someone else can't move your arm), shoulder impingement has good passive ROM (only active motion limited by pain). Pattern frozen shoulder restricted in ALL directions (external rotation most), impingement has painful arc between 60-120 degrees. Stiffness, frozen shoulder has profound 'hard stop' stiffness, impingement has pain limited motion. Onset frozen shoulder gradual progression through 3 stages over months, impingement often related to specific overhead activity. Treatment frozen shoulder focuses on stretching and exercises, impingement focuses on rotator cuff strengthening and postural correction. However, they can coexist chronic untreated impingement can lead to disuse and secondary frozen shoulder.<\/p><\/div><\/div>\n            <\/div><\/div>\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-83bc5c2 e-flex e-con-boxed wpr-particle-no wpr-jarallax-no wpr-parallax-no wpr-sticky-section-no wpr-column-slider-no wpr-equal-height-no e-con e-parent\" data-id=\"83bc5c2\" data-element_type=\"container\" data-e-type=\"container\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"e-con-inner\">\n\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-1f2c778 e-con-full e-flex wpr-particle-no wpr-jarallax-no wpr-parallax-no wpr-sticky-section-no wpr-column-slider-no wpr-equal-height-no e-con e-child\" data-id=\"1f2c778\" data-element_type=\"container\" data-e-type=\"container\">\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-5602227 e-con-full e-flex wpr-particle-no wpr-jarallax-no wpr-parallax-no wpr-sticky-section-no wpr-column-slider-no wpr-equal-height-no e-con e-child\" data-id=\"5602227\" data-element_type=\"container\" data-e-type=\"container\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-142f9b9 elementor-widget elementor-widget-heading\" data-id=\"142f9b9\" data-element_type=\"widget\" data-e-type=\"widget\" data-widget_type=\"heading.default\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-widget-container\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<h2 class=\"elementor-heading-title elementor-size-default\">Related Conditions &amp; Procedures<\/h2>\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-2a21573 e-con-full e-flex wpr-particle-no wpr-jarallax-no wpr